1: # ES Module Lexer
2: 
3: [![Build Status][actions-image]][actions-url]
4: 
5: A JS module syntax lexer used in [es-module-shims](https://github.com/guybedford/es-module-shims).
6: 
7: Outputs the list of exports and locations of import specifiers, including dynamic import and import meta handling.
8: 
9: Supports new syntax features including import attributes and source phase imports.
10: 
11: A very small single JS file (~7KiB gzipped) that includes inlined Web Assembly for very fast source analysis of ECMAScript module syntax only.
12: 
13: For an example of the performance, Angular 1 (720KiB) is fully parsed in 5ms, in comparison to the fastest JS parser, Acorn which takes over 100ms.
14: 
15: _Comprehensively handles the JS language grammar while remaining small and fast. - ~10ms per MB of JS cold and ~5ms per MB of JS warm, [see benchmarks](#benchmarks) for more info._
16: 
17: > [Built with](https://github.com/guybedford/es-module-lexer/blob/main/chompfile.toml) [Chomp](https://chompbuild.com/)
18: 
19: ### Usage
20: 
21: ```
22: npm install es-module-lexer
23: ```
24: 
25: See [src/lexer.ts](src/lexer.ts) for the type definitions.
26: 
27: For use in CommonJS:
28: 
29: ```js
30: const { init, parse } = require('es-module-lexer');
31: 
32: (async () => {
33:   // either await init, or call parse asynchronously
34:   // this is necessary for the Web Assembly boot
35:   await init;
36: 
37:   const source = 'export var p = 5';
38:   const [imports, exports] = parse(source);
39:   
40:   // Returns "p"
41:   source.slice(exports[0].s, exports[0].e);
42:   // Returns "p"
43:   source.slice(exports[0].ls, exports[0].le);
44: })();
45: ```
46: 
47: An ES module version is also available:
48: 
49: ```js
50: import { init, parse } from 'es-module-lexer';
51: 
52: (async () => {
53:   await init;
54: 
55:   const source = `
56:     import { name } from 'mod\\u1011';
57:     import json from './json.json' with { type: 'json' }
58:     export var p = 5;
59:     export function q () {
60: 
61:     };
62:     export { x as 'external name' } from 'external';
63: 
64:     // Comments provided to demonstrate edge cases
65:     import /*comment!*/ (  'asdf', { with: { type: 'json' }});
66:     import /*comment!*/.meta.asdf;
67: 
68:     // Source phase imports:
69:     import source mod from './mod.wasm';
70:     import.source('./mod.wasm');
71:   `;
72: 
73:   const [imports, exports] = parse(source, 'optional-sourcename');
74: 
75:   // Returns "modထ"
76:   imports[0].n
77:   // Returns "mod\u1011"
78:   source.slice(imports[0].s, imports[0].e);
79:   // "s" = start
80:   // "e" = end
81: 
82:   // Returns "import { name } from 'mod'"
83:   source.slice(imports[0].ss, imports[0].se);
84:   // "ss" = statement start
85:   // "se" = statement end
86: 
87:   // Returns "{ type: 'json' }"
88:   source.slice(imports[1].a, imports[1].se);
89:   // "a" = attribute start, -1 for no import attributes
90: 
91:   // Parsed import attributes are available in `at`
92:   // Returns [['type', 'json']]
93:   imports[1].at;
94:   // Returns 'json'
95:   imports[1].at[0][1];
96: 
97:   // Returns null (no attributes)
98:   imports[0].at;
99: 
100:   // Returns "external"
101:   source.slice(imports[2].s, imports[2].e);
102: 
103:   // Returns "p"
104:   source.slice(exports[0].s, exports[0].e);
105:   // Returns "p"
106:   source.slice(exports[0].ls, exports[0].le);
107:   // Returns "q"
108:   source.slice(exports[1].s, exports[1].e);
109:   // Returns "q"
110:   source.slice(exports[1].ls, exports[1].le);
111: 
112:   // "ss" = export statement start (only the start is tracked, not the end)
113:   // Returns "export"
114:   source.slice(exports[0].ss, exports[0].ss + 6);
115: 
116:   // Returns "'external name'"
117:   source.slice(exports[2].s, exports[2].e);
118:   // Returns -1
119:   exports[2].ls;
120:   // Returns -1
121:   exports[2].le;
122: 
123:   // Import type is provided by `t` value
124:   // (1 for static, 2, for dynamic)
125:   // Returns true
126:   imports[2].t == 2;
127: 
128:   // Returns "asdf" (only for string literal dynamic imports)
129:   imports[2].n
130:   // Returns "import /*comment!*/ (  'asdf', { with: { type: 'json' } })"
131:   source.slice(imports[3].ss, imports[3].se);
132:   // Returns "'asdf'"
133:   source.slice(imports[3].s, imports[3].e);
134:   // Returns "(  'asdf', { with: { type: 'json' } })"
135:   source.slice(imports[3].d, imports[3].se);
136:   // Returns "{ with: { type: 'json' } }"
137:   source.slice(imports[3].a, imports[3].se - 1);
138: 
139:   // For non-string dynamic import expressions:
140:   // - n will be undefined
141:   // - a is currently -1 even if there is an import attribute
142:   // - e is currently the character before the closing )
143: 
144:   // For nested dynamic imports, the se value of the outer import is -1 as end tracking does not
145:   // currently support nested dynamic immports
146: 
147:   // import.meta is indicated by imports[3].d === -2
148:   // Returns true
149:   imports[4].d === -2;
150:   // Returns "import /*comment!*/.meta"
151:   source.slice(imports[4].s, imports[4].e);
152:   // ss and se are the same for import meta
153: 
154:   // Returns "'./mod.wasm'"
155:   source.slice(imports[5].s, imports[5].e);
156: 
157:   // Import type 4 and 5 for static and dynamic source phase
158:   imports[5].t === 4;
159:   imports[6].t === 5;
160: })();
161: ```
162: 
163: ### CSP asm.js Build
164: 
165: The default version of the library uses Wasm and (safe) eval usage for performance and a minimal footprint.
166: 
167: Neither of these represent security escalation possibilities since there are no execution string injection vectors, but that can still violate existing CSP policies for applications.
168: 
169: For a version that works with CSP eval disabled, use the `es-module-lexer/js` build:
170: 
171: ```js
172: import { parse } from 'es-module-lexer/js';
173: ```
174: 
175: Instead of Web Assembly, this uses an asm.js build which is almost as fast as the Wasm version ([see benchmarks below](#benchmarks)).
176: 
177: ### Minimal Build
178: 
179: For size-sensitive embedders, the `es-module-lexer/minimal` build drops certain features to reduce the binary size. This is used for example by [es-module-shims](https://github.com/guybedford/es-module-shims):
180: 
181: ```js
182: import { parse } from 'es-module-lexer/minimal';
183: ```
184: 
185: Compared to the full build:
186: 
187: * `parse` returns a two-element `[imports, exports]` tuple only - the third and fourth facade and `hasModuleSyntax` booleans are dropped.
188: * Imports drop the parsed attribute list `at` (the attribute source remains recoverable via the `a` attributes index).
189: * Exports drop the statement start `ss`.
190: 
191: All other fields are identical to the full build. For CSP eval disabled support, the equivalent asm.js build is available as `es-module-lexer/minimal/js`.
192: 
193: ### Import Attributes
194: 
195: The `a` field provides the index of the start of the `{` attributes bracket, or -1 for no attributes.
196: 
197: The list of attribute key and value pairs are provided on the `at` field (full build only):
198: 
199: ```js
200: const [imports] = parse(`
201:   import json from './foo.json' with { type: 'json' };
202:   import './foo.css' with { type: 'css' };
203:   import pkg from 'pkg' with { type: 'json', integrity: 'sha384-...' };
204: `);
205: 
206: // Returns [['type', 'json']]
207: imports[0].at;
208: 
209: // Returns [['type', 'css']]
210: imports[1].at;
211: 
212: // Multiple attributes
213: // Returns [['type', 'json'], ['integrity', 'sha384-...']]
214: imports[2].at;
215: ```
216: 
217: The `at` field is an array of `[key, value]` tuples, or `null` if there are no attributes.
218: 
219: Both keys and values support escape sequences:
220: 
221: ```js
222: const [imports] = parse(`
223:   import foo from './foo.js' with { "custom-key": "value" };
224:   import bar from './bar.js' with { "key\\nwith\\nnewlines": "value\\twith\\ttabs" };
225: `);
226: 
227: // Quoted keys are unquoted
228: // Returns [['custom-key', 'value']]
229: imports[0].at;
230: 
231: // Escape sequences are processed
232: // Returns [['key\nwith\nnewlines', 'value\twith\ttabs']]
233: imports[1].at;
234: ```
235: 
236: ### Escape Sequences
237: 
238: To handle escape sequences in specifier strings, the `.n` field of imported specifiers will be provided where possible.
239: 
240: For dynamic import expressions, this field will be empty if not a valid JS string.
241: 
242: ### Facade Detection
243: 
244: Facade modules that only use import / export syntax can be detected via the third return value (full build only):
245: 
246: ```js
247: const [,, facade] = parse(`
248:   export * from 'external';
249:   import * as ns from 'external2';
250:   export { a as b } from 'external3';
251:   export { ns };
252: `);
253: facade === true;
254: ```
255: 
256: ### ESM Detection
257: 
258: Modules that uses ESM syntaxes can be detected via the fourth return value (full build only):
259: 
260: ```js
261: const [,,, hasModuleSyntax] = parse(`
262:   export {}
263: `);
264: hasModuleSyntax === true;
265: ```
266: 
267: Dynamic imports are ignored since they can be used in Non-ESM files.
268: 
269: ```js
270: const [,,, hasModuleSyntax] = parse(`
271:   import('./foo.js')
272: `);
273: hasModuleSyntax === false;
274: ```
275: 
276: ### Environment Support
277: 
278: Node.js 10+, and [all browsers with Web Assembly support](https://caniuse.com/#feat=wasm).
279: 
280: ### Grammar Support
281: 
282: * Token state parses all line comments, block comments, strings, template strings, blocks, parens and punctuators.
283: * Division operator / regex token ambiguity is handled via backtracking checks against punctuator prefixes, including closing brace or paren backtracking.
284: * Always correctly parses valid JS source, but may parse invalid JS source without errors.
285: 
286: ### Limitations
287: 
288: The lexing approach is designed to deal with the full language grammar including RegEx / division operator ambiguity through backtracking and paren / brace tracking.
289: 
290: Because it lexes rather than fully parses, the analysis is not a validation pass: valid JS source is always analyzed correctly, but some invalid source is accepted without an error rather than rejected. For example `export const = 1` lexes to an empty exports list instead of throwing. Callers that need to reject invalid source should run a validating parser separately.
291: 
292: Multiple exports per declaration (`export var a = 'asdf', q = z`) and renamed destructured exports (`export var { a: b } = asdf`) are detected correctly; earlier versions missed `q` and `b` in these forms.
293: 
